1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,263
In lesson 4.4, the fake stars told you the error bars were too small.

2
00:00:05,263 --> 00:00:09,387
Yet one pixel at a time, the survey's variance map was right.

3
00:00:09,387 --> 00:00:12,750
How can every pixel be right, and their sum be wrong?

4
00:00:13,625 --> 00:00:18,563
Start with nine pixels, each with noise of one unit, independent of each other.

5
00:00:18,563 --> 00:00:20,613
A flux measurement adds them up.

6
00:00:20,613 --> 00:00:26,563
Their wobbles partly cancel, so the sum wobbles by three units: the square root of nine, not nine.

7
00:00:26,563 --> 00:00:29,063
That's the rule an error bar uses.

8
00:00:29,063 --> 00:00:31,850
Add the variances, then take the square root.

9
00:00:32,750 --> 00:00:37,262
Now resample, the way a survey puts every exposure onto its sky grid.

10
00:00:37,262 --> 00:00:40,638
Here xRep shifts pure noise by half a pixel.

11
00:00:40,638 --> 00:00:44,688
Each output pixel is a weighted blend of a few input pixels.

12
00:00:44,688 --> 00:00:51,275
So one input pixel's noise lands in several output pixels, and two neighbours share part of the same noise.

13
00:00:52,175 --> 00:00:57,150
xRep carries the variance along, and pixel by pixel it is exactly right.

14
00:00:57,150 --> 00:01:00,675
But when you add up neighbours, the shared parts don't cancel.

15
00:01:00,675 --> 00:01:02,025
They move together.

16
00:01:02,025 --> 00:01:09,675
So the sum wobbles more than the variances add up to: here, twenty-two percent more, with nothing wrong in any single pixel.

17
00:01:10,550 --> 00:01:13,262
The survey's images show the same thing.

18
00:01:13,262 --> 00:01:21,087
Each pixel shares about a fifth of its noise with the next one, and the bigger the patch you add up, the further the map falls short.

19
00:01:21,087 --> 00:01:25,375
A map with one number per pixel can't record how pixels move together.

20
00:01:25,375 --> 00:01:30,050
So you measure the factor on the image itself, and widen the error bars by it.
